00:14:26.460 --> 00:14:28.770
So about that story I promised you.

00:14:28.770 --> 00:14:36.090
There are hundreds of rags to riches stories,
each one is inspiring in its own way but there

00:14:36.090 --> 00:14:43.220
is one entrepreneur who has perhaps suffered
more than any other on his journey to success.

00:14:43.220 --> 00:14:53.200
Californian John Paul DeJoria is today worth
$4 billion, but not so long ago he was homeless.

00:14:53.200 --> 00:14:56.340
Let me tell you exactly how that happened.

00:14:56.340 --> 00:15:01.500
DeJoria grew up in a very poor household without
a father, at nine years old he started selling

00:15:01.500 --> 00:15:05.640
newspapers so his mother and brother could
eat.

00:15:05.640 --> 00:15:10.100
Eventually DeJoria was sent to a foster home
when his mother could no longer support him

00:15:10.100 --> 00:15:11.270
and his brother.

00:15:11.270 --> 00:15:16.779
In school his maths teacher told him “he
would never succeed at anything in life”

00:15:16.779 --> 00:15:22.160
and DeJoria says that ever since that moment
he was determined to change his life.

00:15:22.160 --> 00:15:28.350
An interesting side note, my maths teacher
once told me the exact same thing, seriously,

00:15:28.350 --> 00:15:29.820
what is wrong with maths teachers?

00:15:29.820 --> 00:15:32.110
Also, if you’re watching haha!

00:15:32.110 --> 00:15:38.890
But if only things were that simple for DeJoria,
because from here on out things only got worse

00:15:38.890 --> 00:15:39.920
for him.

00:15:39.920 --> 00:15:45.530
At 22 DeJoria's wife abandoned him and their
two-year-old son, but not before she secretly

00:15:45.530 --> 00:15:50.350
drained every dime from his bank account and
took his only car.

00:15:50.350 --> 00:15:54.770
It’s not really my place to say, but wow,
what a grade A bitch!

00:15:54.770 --> 00:15:59.570
DeJoria was subsequently evicted from their
flat and forced to live on the streets with

00:15:59.570 --> 00:16:00.830
his infant son.

00:16:00.830 --> 00:16:05.970
He was homeless for some time and resorted
to rummaging through bins to find empty bottles.

00:16:05.970 --> 00:16:10.960
At this time you could trade in empty bottle
at grocery stores for a couple of cents.

00:16:10.960 --> 00:16:16.460
He did this for a while to make money to buy
food for his son.

00:16:16.460 --> 00:16:19.740
Eventually DeJoria got a job as a door to
door salesman.

00:16:19.740 --> 00:16:25.190
The job requirements must have listed “must
make a good stereotype” because he sold

00:16:25.190 --> 00:16:26.830
encyclopedias.

00:16:26.830 --> 00:16:31.610
One day DeJoria decided to start a hair care
company with his friend Paul Mitchell, who

00:16:31.610 --> 00:16:33.900
was also broke.

00:16:33.900 --> 00:16:39.830
Incredibly they managed to secure a $500,000
investment from a venture capitalist, but

00:16:39.830 --> 00:16:45.870
on the day they were supposed to launch the
company the only investor pulled out, leaving

00:16:45.870 --> 00:16:48.490
them with nothing.

00:16:48.490 --> 00:16:54.370
This forced DeJoria to become homeless once
again, he began living out of his car.

00:16:54.370 --> 00:17:00.839
So in one last ditch attempt to make his hair
care company a success, he and his business

00:17:00.839 --> 00:17:06.220
partner Paul Mitchell pooled all their money
together, and DeJoria asked his mum for a

00:17:06.220 --> 00:17:08.319
loan of $300.

00:17:08.319 --> 00:17:11.379
All of this added up to just $700.

00:17:11.379 --> 00:17:16.740
But it was just enough to produce their first
bottles of shampoo, they even had to print

00:17:16.740 --> 00:17:20.899
the packaging in black and white because they
couldn’t afford colour.

00:17:20.899 --> 00:17:27.049
According to DeJoria, during the first two
years they nearly went bankrupt almost every

00:17:27.049 --> 00:17:34.940
day, but eventually they managed to slowly
build up the business and their customer base

00:17:34.940 --> 00:17:36.950
of salons.

00:17:36.950 --> 00:17:42.669
After two years they were able to finally
pay their bills and focus on growing the business.

00:17:42.669 --> 00:17:50.659
Their brand John Paul Mitchell Systems became
a global success, selling in 150,000 salons

00:17:50.659 --> 00:17:52.400
all over the globe.

00:17:52.400 --> 00:18:01.779
John Paul DeJoria had everything taken from
him twice and was homeless twice, today the

00:18:01.779 --> 00:18:06.039
man is worth $4 billion.

00:18:06.039 --> 00:18:11.470
Off the back of his success in hair care also
started a premium Tequila company called Patron

00:18:11.470 --> 00:18:18.020
Spirits, which he just recently sold to Bacardi
for $5.1 billion.

00:18:18.020 --> 00:18:23.870
Now if DeJoria’s story doesn’t give you
the determination to stick to and pursue your

00:18:23.870 --> 00:18:27.049
dreams no matter what, then nothing will.

00:18:27.049 --> 00:18:32.929
At the end of the day, whether you’re university
educated or not, there’s only one person

00:18:32.929 --> 00:18:37.119
that can make you successful, you.

00:18:37.119 --> 00:18:42.990
And it won’t be your college or university
diploma that’s responsible, it will be your

00:18:42.990 --> 00:18:49.500
determination to make it happen.

00:18:49.500 --> 00:19:02.030
Thanks for watching.

